<p>20 years ago, nearly 15% of the undergraduate degrees conferred in the United States were in the humanities.<span id="more-58327"></span></p>
<p>As of 2025, that percentage is down to 8.1%.</p>
<p>These figures are from a recent <a href="https://www.amacad.org/humanities-indicators/higher-education/bachelors-degrees-humanities" target="_blank" rel="noopener">report</a> by <a href="https://www.amacad.org/humanities-indicators" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Humanities Indicators</a>, a project of the <a href="https://www.amacad.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">American Academy of Arts &amp; Sciences</a>.</p>
<p>Here are the numbers:</p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>In 2025, the nation’s colleges and universities awarded 164,847 bachelor’s degrees in the humanities&#8212;the smallest number of degrees awarded since 1991 and 30.4% below the recent high-water mark in 2012&#8230; That decline includes a particularly sharp drop of 12.7% from 2021 to 2023, followed by a more modest drop of 2.1% from 2023 to 2024, and a further decline of just 0.4% from 2024 to 2025—the smallest year-on-year decline since 2012.</em></p>
<p>And most disciplines within the humanities have shown a decline:</p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>Within the humanities, almost every discipline awarded fewer degrees in 2025 than 13 years earlier&#8230; The largest proportional drops in degrees awarded occurred in religious studies (down 56.3%), area studies (53.2%), languages and literatures other than English (50.8%), English (43.8%), history (40.6%), classical studies (39.4%), and comparative literature (31%). Only three disciplines showed increases&#8212;folklore (where the small number of degrees awarded makes the numbers highly volatile), the study of the arts (with a modest 2.1% increase since 2012), and interdisciplinary humanities disciplines (where the increase was largely due to the addition of a substantial number of new programs, such as digital humanities, in the Department of Education’s 2020 revision of its Classification of Instructional Programs).</em></p>
<p>How&#8217;s philosophy doing? Well, after a <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/05/11/slight-decrease-in-us-philosophy-majors-continues/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">slight decrease last year</a>, there has been a slight increase:</p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="size-large wp-image-58330 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/degree-completion-by-discplines-2025-9-humanities-indicators-1024x849.png" alt="" width="1024" height="849" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/degree-completion-by-discplines-2025-9-humanities-indicators-1024x849.png 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/degree-completion-by-discplines-2025-9-humanities-indicators-300x249.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/degree-completion-by-discplines-2025-9-humanities-indicators-768x637.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/degree-completion-by-discplines-2025-9-humanities-indicators-400x332.png 400w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/degree-completion-by-discplines-2025-9-humanities-indicators.png 1314w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></p>
<p>In 2024, 5,763 students earned philosophy BAs. In 2025, that number was 5,918.</p>
